Lucinda Barlow’s team gets smaller every year. So does her budget. Yet her priorities keep growing, and she says the same is true for every CMO.

Speaking at the humAIn conference in Sydney, days after Uber Eats and Special Australia took the Grand Effie at the Australian Effie Awards, Uber’s head of international marketing said that pressure pushes teams towards systems thinking and AI-fuelled efficiency. They find what works, then codify it, scale it and automate it.

It’s a mindset borrowed from tech. “When you work in technology, you hear the language that becomes culture,” she said. “Fast is better than slow. Frictionless, effortless, seamless. Fail fast. This language becomes the discipline of business, and it becomes this truth.”

The risk, she said, is that creativity drains away. Push too far down that road and panic sets in, bringing “an irrational desire for rationality”.

